# The party hub — the class and identifier vocabulary that source packages in
# the Semvia demo quiver align into.
#
# WHY A HUB. Alignment between N independently-authored packages is O(N²) if
# each package aligns to each other package directly: five source packages that
# all describe organizations need ten bridges, and every new package needs five
# more. Aligning each package to a hub instead is O(N) — one alignment per
# package, authored once, and a package added later costs one file and touches
# nothing that already shipped. The saving is not merely arithmetic: a pairwise
# bridge COUPLES the two packages it spans, so one side cannot re-version its
# IRIs without breaking the other, while a hub alignment couples each package
# only to a vocabulary that is deliberately small and deliberately stable.
#
# WHY IT IS THIS SMALL. Every term here is one a source package will align to
# and a query will read. The hub is not a place to model parties well — it is
# the narrowest vocabulary that lets two packages meet. A term nothing aligns to
# is a term that costs rewrite budget at read time and buys nothing, so terms
# are added when a second package needs them, not in anticipation.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY OMITS. No addresses, no jurisdictions, no relationships,
# no roles. Those differ enough between sources that a hub term would either be
# so loose it says nothing or so tight it misrepresents one side. Sources keep
# their own; a query that needs them reads the source vocabulary directly.
#
# House modelling rules this file follows (monorepo CLAUDE.md, "Inference
# Layer"):
#   - predicates are bare `rdf:Property` with label + comment, carrying NO
#     `rdfs:domain` / `rdfs:range`. Those are live typing declarations under
#     rdfs2/rdfs3 as of #4242, and a hub is the worst possible place to put one:
#     a range here would type the objects of every aligned source's data.
#   - no `sh:property` constraints on hub classes. A hub class is reached by
#     alignment from data the hub does not control and cannot validate — see
#     the "third-party vocabularies" note in CLAUDE.md. Constraining it would
#     turn another package's ordinary data into violations in this one.

@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix sh:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix svs:   <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/meta-schema#> .
@prefix svds:  <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/dataset#> .
@prefix party: <#> .

<> a svs:Schema ;
  dct:title "Party"@en ;
  dct:description "Classes and canonical identifiers for organizations and people, as an alignment target. The hub of the demo quiver's hub-and-spoke alignment topology."@en ;
  svds:imports <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/meta-schema> ;
  sh:declare [
    a sh:PrefixDeclaration ;
    sh:prefix "party" ;
    sh:namespace party:
  ] ;
.

################################################################
# Classes
################################################################

# The top of the hub lattice. Named `Party` rather than `Agent` — `Agent` is the
# RDF-conventional term (FOAF), but in this organization it reads as "AI agent"
# on first encounter, and a hub term is read far more often than it is written.
#
# A Party is anything that can hold an identifier and enter a relationship: a
# company, a person, a fund, a government body. It is deliberately broader than
# any single source's top class, because it has to sit above all of them.
party:Party a rdfs:Class ;
  rdfs:label "Party"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "An organization or a person — anything that can be identified and can hold a relationship to another party. The alignment target a source package's top-level actor class points at."@en ;
.

# NOT `owl:equivalentClass foaf:Organization` or `org:FormalOrganization`, and
# not `rdfs:subClassOf` either. Both are adopt-as-DEPENDENCY moves, and the
# discriminator is deployment: nobody's data in this quiver is minted with FOAF
# or W3C ORG IRIs, so importing them would buy interoperability with no actual
# corpus while adding a vocabulary to every install. They are adopted as
# INFLUENCE — this class means what `foaf:Organization` means, and a consumer
# who needs the edge asserts it in their own workspace, where it costs only them.
party:Organization a rdfs:Class ;
  rdfs:subClassOf party:Party ;
  rdfs:label "Organization"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "A party that is not a natural person: a company, a fund, a government body, a nonprofit, a branch. Deliberately does not distinguish among these — a source that draws the distinction keeps its own subclasses and aligns them here."@en ;
.

party:Person a rdfs:Class ;
  rdfs:subClassOf party:Party ;
  rdfs:label "Person"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "A natural person."@en ;
.

################################################################
# Canonical identifiers
################################################################
#
# These are the hub's real work, and the reason it dissolves bridges rather than
# merely relocating them. Two packages that both carry the LEI do not need to
# know about each other AT ALL once both align their LEI predicate to
# `party:lei`: `owl:equivalentProperty` is walked in both directions at read
# time (#4986), so one pattern on `party:lei` reaches both datasets, and the
# join key is a value both sources already publish rather than a mapping table
# somebody has to maintain.
#
# The test for admitting an identifier here is whether it is issued by a
# STANDARDS BODY rather than by one of the sources. An LEI is ISO 17442 and
# every LEI holder has the same one everywhere; a vendor's internal customer id
# is not, and putting one here would make the hub a party to one source's
# namespace.

party:lei a rdf:Property ;
  rdfs:label "LEI"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "The ISO 17442 Legal Entity Identifier, as the twenty-character literal the issuer publishes — not as an IRI and not as a reified identifier object. The canonical join key between any two packages that carry it."@en ;
.

party:name a rdf:Property ;
  rdfs:label "name"@en ;
  rdfs:comment "A name the party is known by. Deliberately loose: it is the union target that a source's registered-legal-name, trading-name and transliteration predicates all align UP into via rdfs:subPropertyOf, so that one pattern surfaces every name without asserting that any of them is the legal one. A source that needs the legal name specifically reads its own predicate."@en ;
.
